<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Referral for the concerns below, which began before the index visit. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Low mood, loss of interest, and poor sleep are affecting legal work and friendships. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Abraham Lincoln was born February 12, 1809, near what is now Hodgenville, Kentucky. [D]</item><item>In 1859 Lincoln described himself as &quot;six feet, four inches, nearly&quot; in height. [D]</item><item>In his tenth year Lincoln was kicked by a horse and, in his own account, &quot;apparently killed for a time.&quot; [D]</item><item>On January 23, 1841, Lincoln wrote to John T. Stuart, &quot;I am now the most miserable man living.&quot; [D]</item><item>Lincoln’s recurrent melancholy is retrospectively interpreted by modern clinicians as chronic depression that did not prevent him from leading the nation. [R]</item><item>Lincoln’s mother, Nancy Hanks Lincoln, died of milk sickness on October 5, 1818, when he was nine. [D]</item><item>Marfan syndrome, or another inherited marfanoid disorder such as multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2B, has been proposed retrospectively from Lincoln’s build; the diagnosis is disputed and has never been genetically confirmed. [R]</item><item>Lincoln’s older sister, Sarah Lincoln Grigsby, died on January 20, 1828, at age twenty-one, from complications during delivery that also took her infant’s life. [D]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

